**Corollary 25.2 (Literal determinant differences).** Suppose $\gamma$ has a centered representative of size $O(K)$. Outside the subfamily $n'=n$ and $(m'-m)\times n=p\gamma\times n$ as integers, its contribution is $O(D_0K^4/p)$. In particular, at $\gamma=0$ the contribution outside the exact literal determinant slices has this bound. Summing all $O(K^3)$ such centered differences costs $O(D_0K^7/p)$ outside the prescribed relations.

**Proof.** The case $k\ne0$ already has the required estimate. For $k=0$ the vectors $(n_i,p\gamma_i-(m_i'-m_i))$ are in the index-$q$ graph lattice of $c$ and have norm $O(L)$. If $T_c$ is smaller than a sufficiently small constant times $q/L$, their determinants with a shortest vector have size less than $q$ and are divisible by $q$. They vanish as integers. The shortest vector has nonzero first coordinate, so $p\gamma-(m'-m)$ is rationally parallel to $n$. Thus outside the prescribed relation one can take $B$ comparable to $q/L$ in the $k=0$ estimate, which gives $O(D_0(K^3/p+K/p))$. Add the bound for $k\ne0$.

The projected-difference estimate permits any $O(Kp^{1/6-59\delta/18})$ two-dimensional differences in the projected moment budget. This follows by substituting $D_0=L^6/p^2$ into $p^6(pL^{-10}M_g)^{1/2}$, the bound from Cauchy–Schwarz over $\rho$ for the transverse sum. The exact phases are still needed across the rest of the projected readouts.

